{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7JioWRmKqWZfTshmy2g","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've used your $75k cost cut to show what break-even would take, and I've stated plainly that you don't have a profitability date yet. I tied the $600k to getting through the 2027 pollination season, because that's when you'll learn whether customers come back and whether the $45 sensor costs you business.\n\n<final_document>\n**Subject: [Company] half-year update, and a bridge round we'd like you in**\n\nHi all,\n\nIn short: we're growing, we have new competition, and we're raising a $600,000 bridge to get us through the 2027 pollination season. I'd like each of you to take part. Details are at the end.\n\n**Customers and revenue**\nWe have 31 paying beekeepers, up from 12 a year ago. Revenue for the half-year was $212,000. Our sales are very seasonal: 70% came in March and April, when beekeepers get their hives ready for pollination. Each sensor costs us $38 to make and sells for $55. On top of that, the app brings in $4 per hive per month.\n\n**What we're seeing in the field**\nOur largest customer runs 4,000 hives. Over the winter he lost 18% of his colonies, compared with 31% the winter before. He says the sensors were part of that, but he also changed his mite treatment, so we can't say how much of the improvement came from us. We find it encouraging, but it isn't proof.\n\n**Competition**\nIn May a large farm equipment company launched a similar sensor at $45, which is $10 below ours. Three of our beekeepers have asked to test it. Their sensor measures only temperature. Ours also measures sound and hive weight, and that's how we pick up a weak colony. We don't yet know whether that difference outweighs the price for our customers. This spring's orders will tell us, and I'll report back on what we learn.\n\n**Cash and costs**\nWe spend about $95,000 a month and have $340,000 in the bank. By February we'll bring spending down to $75,000 a month by not filling two open roles.\n\nWe're not profitable, and I won't give you a date for it until we have a model I'd stand behind. Here's the arithmetic: at $75,000 a month, we break even at about $900,000 a year in revenue. That means a half-year like this one would need to bring in about $450,000, a little more than double what we did. Whether we get there depends on how many customers come back and grow this spring, and on how we hold up against the $45 sensor.\n\n**Why the bridge, and what it buys**\nWithout new money, and counting spending alone, we'd run out of cash around the end of March. That's in the middle of the season that brings in most of our revenue, and we'd be depending on those sales to stay open.\n\nWith the $600,000 and the cost cut, we have cash into late 2027 before counting any revenue. That takes us through the 2027 pollination season, which is the test that matters. It will show whether customers renew and grow, and whether the cheaper competitor costs us business. After the season we'll have real numbers to build a profitability plan on and to decide on a next raise.\n\n**Terms**\n- Convertible note, 20% discount, $6M cap\n- $250,000 already committed by an angel investor; $350,000 still open\n- Closes December 15\n\nIf you'd like to take part, please reply to me by **December 1** with the amount. I'm happy to talk it through before then.\n\nThanks for backing us,\n[Name]\n</final_document>\n\nBefore you send it, check these points:\n\n- **Runway:** I treated $95k a month as total spending and ignored revenue. On that basis, the bridge plus the cut lasts until about November 2027, and without the bridge cash runs out around the end of March. If $95k is net burn instead, both dates move.\n- **Which months the half-year covers:** The $212k half-year apparently includes March and April. Make sure it matches the reporting period your investors expect.\n- **Numbers investors will ask for:** If you can find how many hives pay the monthly app fee and how many of last year's 12 beekeepers renewed, add them under \"Customers and revenue.\" They're the strongest evidence you could give against the competitor worry."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1316,"cache_read_input_tokens":9828,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1316},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c0db689-b97e-4566-afcc-7c9a4412c124","uuid":"3a6aa537-b53c-4b82-b201-3c033a4d3f2a","timestamp":"2026-11-16T21:20:53.203Z","request_id":"req_011Ch7JioGYZfRESEPQufUZG"}
